{
  "requirement": "When given `--path <crate-dir>` and no existing Habeas dev dependency, `habeas setup` MUST add that local crate as the dev dependency. Repeating setup MUST NOT add a duplicate Habeas dependency.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Implementation (`src/setup.rs`)**\n- `run` parses the project's `Cargo.toml` and calls `has_habeas_dev_dependency`. That helper checks whether `[dev-dependencies]` has a `habeas` entry.\n- Only if there is no such entry does `run` execute `cargo add --dev habeas --manifest-path <Cargo.toml>`.\n- When `--path` is supplied, `run` appends `--path <canonicalized local crate>`. So the local crate becomes the dev dependency, and the check prevents a second addition. This matches both halves of the requirement.\n- Both the helper and the call site carry `{impl}` annotations for `requirements.cli.setup_local_path`.\n- Failure of `cargo add` is reported as an error.\n\n**Tests (`tests/setup.rs`, `setup_is_repeatable`)**\n- The test is annotated with `{test}` for `requirements.cli.setup_local_path`.\n- It creates a fixture project with no Habeas dependency.\n- It runs `habeas setup --path $CARGO_MANIFEST_DIR` twice, with `CARGO_NET_OFFLINE` set, so the local path must be what gets used.\n- It then asserts that `Cargo.toml` contains `[dev-dependencies]`, `habeas` and `path = `.\n- The second run exercises the \"existing dependency\" branch. A duplicate entry would produce a duplicate TOML key. That would make the second `cargo add` or the manifest parse fail, and the helper `run` asserts success.\n- Many other tests (`tests/setup.rs` lines 114–204, `tests/symposium_hook_smoke.py`) also invoke `setup --path` and require success.\n- `tests/cli.rs` covers argument validation.\n\n**Minor weaknesses, none acknowledged as FIXMEs**\n- The test does not assert that the `path` value equals the local crate directory.\n- It does not assert that `habeas` appears exactly once.\n- It does not test a pre-existing registry dependency combined with `--path`.\n\nThese gaps are covered indirectly by the points above, and the behavior is simple. The requirement is adequately implemented and tested.",
  "inputs": [
